---
import Post from '../layouts/Post.astro';
import DeliveryExplorer from '../components/DeliveryExplorer.astro';
import katex from 'katex';
const math=(s:string)=>katex.renderToString(s,{displayMode:true,throwOnError:true});
---
<Post title="Guaranteed delivery despite a pursuer" description="How many indirect journeys can a directed network guarantee? Extremal bounds, sparse optimal networks, dense constructions, and verified strategies." updated="2026-09-13">
  <p class="note">Research checkpoint: explicit composition lowers the complete main-interval threshold to 115; dense and sparse constructions and support bounds are strengthened. The vertex maximum is exact at every order from twelve. Both arc maxima and the full joint maximum are determined in stated eventual ranges; finite exceptions remain.</p>
  <p>A messenger has obtained a message at one station of a directed network. A pursuer starts at a different station. Both can see each other, and they take turns moving along an arrow or waiting. The messenger moves first. Can the message reach its recipient before interception, whatever the pursuer does?</p>
  <p>A direct arrow to the recipient makes delivery immediate. We count the <strong>additional source–recipient pairs without a direct arrow</strong> for which delivery is guaranteed from every allowed pursuer starting position. A network can support almost all such journeys with surprisingly few connections.</p>
  <p><a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/">Detailed proofs</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/">Lean verification</a> · <a href="/cop-prisoner/paper/">Paper and sources</a></p>
  <DeliveryExplorer />
  <h2>The rules that matter</h2>
  <p>The graph is finite and directed, with no loops or repeated arcs; opposite arrows are allowed. Both players may wait. The messenger must deliver in finite time: avoiding capture forever does not count. It observes the pursuer's starting position and may choose its strategy accordingly.</p>
  <p>Receipt ends the game immediately, even if the pursuer occupies the recipient. This models an alert that cannot be cancelled once it arrives. At every other station, collision means interception. Reaching an intermediate station is therefore different from reaching the recipient: the pursuer gets another turn before the message can move onward.</p>
  <h2>What we maximize</h2>
  <p>Write <i>I(G)</i> for the number of guaranteed indirect pairs in a graph <i>G</i>. We study three maxima: <i>M<sub>V</sub>(n)</i> fixes the number of stations, <i>M<sub>E</sub>(m)</i> fixes the number of arcs, and <i>M(n,m)</i> fixes both. Isolated stations are allowed.</p>
  <p>For every graph on <i>n ≥ 3</i> stations,</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`I(G)\le n(n-3).`)} />
  <p>The proof counts one recipient at a time. If it has no incoming arrow, it cannot be reached. If it has just one predecessor, the pursuer camps there. With two or more predecessors, at most <i>n − 3</i> sources remain eligible to count as indirect.</p>
  <h2>Sparse networks that attain the bound</h2>
  <p><strong>For every <i>n ≥ 12</i>, the exact vertex maximum is <i>n(n − 3)</i>.</strong> There is an explicit network with two outgoing and two incoming arrows at every station in which every pair admits guaranteed delivery. Subtract the diagonal pairs and its <i>2n</i> direct arrows to obtain</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M_V(n)=M_E(2n)=M(n,2n)=n(n-3),\qquad n\ge12.`)} />
  <p>The uniform construction starts with a repeating strip of six station types, joined into a cycle. We can remove selected stations and reconnect their incoming and outgoing arrows. Spacing those removals gives every possible remainder when the total number of stations is divided by six.</p>
  <figure><img src="/cop-prisoner/figures/six-type-removal.svg" alt="Two colored paths through a station before removal, and two separate direct paths after reconnecting each color" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Removing one station preserves the two incoming and two outgoing arrows at every remaining station. A separate strategy proof shows why these changes also preserve guaranteed delivery.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>A finite local strategy advances the messenger safely by one column. Repeating it brings the messenger near the recipient, where a second strategy delivers. An artificial outside pursuer state includes every possible entry into the local window. Two independent programs check all local strategies, and a mathematical projection argument proves they work on every sufficiently long cycle. This gives every order from 85; complete finite certificates supply orders 12 through 84.</p>
  <p>The 12-station example in the explorer also has a complete Lean proof of all its delivery guarantees. The general repeating construction is proved by ordinary arguments and independently checked certificates; it is not yet formalized in Lean.</p>
  <h2>Fixing the number of connections</h2>
  <p>Even and odd arc counts have slightly different exact answers:</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M_E(2k)=k(k-3)\quad(k\ge12),\qquad M_E(2k+1)=k(k-3)+2\quad(k\ge12).`)} />
  <p>Even equality uses the two-in/two-out construction on <i>k</i> stations. Odd equality uses <i>k + 1</i> stations with two exceptions: one station has just one outgoing arrow, and another has just one incoming arrow. These force losses. Six modified repeating constructions guarantee every remaining eligible pair and reach the upper bound exactly.</p>
  <p>The odd proof includes a case where a safe phase advances by either one or two columns. Allowing both advances is essential: insisting that every phase end at one prescribed column would reject a valid construction. Delivery modules from both nearby columns complete the proof. The displayed formula covers every odd budget from 25 onward. Six small witnesses and a longer finite bridge connect the uniform construction to this threshold.</p>
  <h2>The entire main interval</h2>
  <p><strong>Angel's full-interval conjecture is proved:</strong> for every sufficiently large <i>n</i>, every integer budget between <i>2n</i> and <i>n(n − 3)</i> attains every missing pair.</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,m)=n(n-1)-m,\qquad2n\le m\le n(n-3).`)} />
  <p>The proof combines several constructions. A repeating local strategy handles the first sparse budgets. A modular construction then allows many added arrows while protecting only the neighborhood differences needed by its strategy. A new composition using incomparable hub subsets then connects this range to the dense complement constructions.</p>
  <p>The complete interval now follows from explicit combinatorial recipes. Every integer budget is obtained by selecting the required number of optional arrows. A separate probabilistic routing theorem remains useful when the routing set and the density of arrows touching it are prescribed.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Every <i>n ≥ 12</i>: the exact sparse endpoint <i>m = 2n</i>.</li>
    <li>Every <i>n ≥ 115</i>: every budget <i>2n ≤ m ≤ 3n</i>, with arbitrary subsets of the permitted added arrows and delivery within <i>9⌈n/6⌉ + 21</i> moves.</li>
    <li>Every <i>n ≥ 25</i>: every budget <i>3n ≤ m ≤ n(⌈n/7⌉ − 1)</i>, with arbitrary subsets of the permitted added arrows.</li>
    <li>Every <i>n ≥ 115</i>: the entire main interval <i>2n ≤ m ≤ n(n − 3)</i>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The threshold 115 is sufficient; its minimal possible value remains open. The sparse recipe has longer delivery strategies. The antichain and dense recipes guarantee two moves. These are distinct guarantees even where their budget intervals overlap.</p>
  <h2>Composition through incomparable hub sets</h2>
  <p>Start with a small core of two types of hub. For every outside station <i>x</i>, specify a set <i>Kₓ</i> of hubs it cannot reach. Every hub can reach every outside station. Choose the sets so that no one contains another. If both the messenger and pursuer are outside, there is a hub the messenger can reach that the pursuer cannot. The messenger safely uses that hub and delivers on its next move.</p>
  <figure><img src="/cop-prisoner/figures/antichain-routing.svg" alt="Two incomparable missing-hub sets leave hub P3 accessible to the source but inaccessible to the pursuer, giving a safe route to the recipient" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Incomparability supplies a safe intermediate hub. The theorem also handles recipients and pursuers inside the core.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>The full theorem imposes simple spacing restrictions within each hub type and requires each set to have at least three members. With <i>2k</i> hubs, <i>q</i> outside stations, and total set size <i>W</i>, it permits <strong>every subset of arrows between outside stations</strong>, throughout the exact interval</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`A\le m\le A+q(q-1),\qquad A=2k(2k-3)+4kq-W.`)} />
  <p>The sets can be produced by choosing separated positions on a cycle. Their number grows exponentially with the core size. Consequently a core of logarithmic size suffices for arbitrarily many outside stations. This is <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#composition-through-incomparable-hub-sets">Theorem 59</a>; it supplies the new connection needed for the main-interval result.</p>
  <h2>A protected routing set: one construction, an interval of budgets</h2>
  <p>For <i>n ≥ 4</i>, reserve <i>b ≤ n</i> of the stations for routing. There are <i>Q = b(2n − b − 1)</i> possible arrows touching that set. Choose an integer <i>0 &lt; a &lt; Q</i> of them and put <i>p = a/Q</i>. If</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`(b-3)p^2(1-p)>4\log n+\log(8/3),`)} />
  <p>a deterministic polynomial algorithm chooses those arrows so that <strong>every choice of the remaining exterior arrows</strong> preserves delivery within two moves. This includes an adversarial choice. Therefore the same protected arrows attain every exact budget</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`a\le m\le a+(n-b)(n-b-1).`)} />
  <p>Taking all stations as routing stations recovers the former exact-budget theorem, its algorithm, and its two-move guarantee. Taking fewer stations adds freedom that the earlier theorem did not provide. The proof also gives an explicit random-sampling success bound. This is <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#protected-routing-sets-at-every-exact-budget">Theorem 54</a>.</p>
  <p>The construction also identifies the right scale of the smallest budget for <em>every</em> pair to admit delivery within two moves. Calling that budget <i>q₂(n)</i>,</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`q_2(n)=\Theta(n\log n).`)} />
  <p>A separating-system inequality gives the lower bound below. The antichain construction gives the improved upper bound, with an explicit parameter rule for every <i>n ≥ 30</i>:</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`\left\lceil\frac n2(\log_2n-1)\right\rceil\le q_2(n)\le(5.3555763082\ldots+o(1))n\log_2n.`)} />
  <p>The exact first budget and its optimal leading constant remain open. An order-<i>n</i> budget can still guarantee every pair if longer delivery is allowed; the logarithmic factor is the price established here for requiring two moves.</p>
  <h2>Dense constructions with freely chosen missing arrows</h2>
  <p>For every <i>n ≥ 16</i>, a fixed set of <i>2n</i> missing arrows and <i>n</i> optional missing arrows guarantee every missing pair in two moves, whichever optional subset is chosen. Larger explicit envelopes keep the same freedom. If <i>n = 10q + s</i>, <i>q ≥ 3</i>, <i>0 ≤ s ≤ 9</i>, every missing count from <i>2n</i> through <i>10q² + 5q + s² + s</i> works. If <i>n = 4q + s</i>, <i>q ≥ 11</i>, <i>0 ≤ s ≤ 3</i>, the endpoint is <i>4q² − 26q + s² + s</i>. Use the larger endpoint when both apply.</p>
  <p>The second recipe reaches <i>n²/4 − O(n)</i> missing arrows. This improves the earlier <i>n²/13</i> range while preserving two-move delivery and every optional-subset choice. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#direct-missing-graphs-unify-the-dense-constructions">proof</a> checks a fixed list of possible blocked intermediaries using residues and parity.</p>
  <h2>The complete final dense band</h2>
  <p>An added arrow also helps the pursuer. In the explorer's diamond, adding just one connection destroys the only indirect guarantee. More generally, above <i>n(n − 2)</i> arrows every indirect guarantee disappears. Exactly at that boundary, for <i>n ≥ 2</i>,</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M(n,n(n-2))=\left\lfloor\frac{n-2}{2}\right\rfloor.`)} />
  <p>At the slightly lower density <i>n(n − 3)</i>, we have a very different answer: at every <i>n ≥ 10</i>, all <i>2n</i> missing arrows correspond to guaranteed indirect journeys. Hence <i>M(n,n(n − 3)) = 2n</i>. The construction has two types of station, with two modularly specified missing arrows per station. An explicit insertion supplies odd orders. The modular proof handles every order except eleven; an explicit 11-station core closes that case and is also checked in Lean.</p>
  <p>For <strong>every <i>n ≥ 20</i></strong>, the entire range from that density through the last nonzero value is now classified. Write <i>r</i> for the number of additional arrows beyond <i>n(n − 3)</i>. Then</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`M\bigl(n,n(n-3)+r\bigr)=2n-r-\left\lceil\frac r2\right\rceil-1,\qquad 1\le r\le n.`)} />
  <p>Every value has an explicit attaining construction. At <i>r = 0</i>, all <i>2n</i> missing pairs win. The next added arrow forces two further failures, leaving <i>2n − 3</i> guarantees. The remaining values descend in a staircase to <i>⌊(n − 2)/2⌋</i>, after which the maximum is zero.</p>
  <figure><img src="/cop-prisoner/figures/dense-staircase.svg" alt="Exact final dense-band maximum for 100 stations, decreasing from 200 to 49 guarantees as 100 arrows are added; the simple missing-pair count remains strictly above it" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Every value on the solid curve is proved exact. The dashed line counts missing arrows but does not account for forced failures.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>The proof counts vertices with exactly one missing outgoing arrow. Their missing neighbors lose whole source rows, while repeated missing neighbors create further recipient obstructions. Functional blocks and the new two-type cores attain the resulting bound; a four-station attachment handles the first step already at every <i>n ≥ 16</i>. Every counted pair in these constructions delivers within two moves. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/">detailed proofs</a> give the constructions and the exact finite-certificate dependencies.</p>
  <h2>Which sparse shapes can still compete?</h2>
  <p>The new support theorems control near-maximizers as well as exact maximizers. With <i>2k</i> arrows, for every <i>k ≥ 6</i>, either deleting isolated stations leaves exactly <i>k</i> stations with two incoming and two outgoing arrows each, or</p>
  <div set:html={math(String.raw`I(G)\le(k-1)(k-3)=k(k-3)-(k-3).`)} />
  <p>Departing from the regular shape therefore costs at least <i>k − 3</i> guarantees relative to the eventual optimum. A regular shape still needs a strategy proof. For the small unresolved budgets, the theorem gives the rigorous upper bounds <i>M<sub>E</sub>(12) ≤ 15</i>, <i>M<sub>E</sub>(14) ≤ 24</i>, <i>M<sub>E</sub>(16) ≤ 35</i>, and <i>M<sub>E</sub>(22) ≤ 81</i>; these are not claimed exact.</p>
  <p>With <i>2k + 1</i> arrows and <i>k ≥ 3</i>, a graph scoring more than <i>k² − 4k + 6</i> reduces to an induced <i>k</i>-station core with at least the same score, or one of three precisely specified degree patterns on <i>k + 1</i> stations. This general theorem replaces the special support analysis previously needed for the 23-arrow result. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#a-quantitative-reduction-for-sparse-near-extremizers">proofs of Theorems 56–57</a> state the patterns and justify the reduction in the actual game.</p>
  <p>A new quantitative theorem continues below those exact-support cutoffs. Put <i>η = m − 2√I(G)</i>. Then the nonisolated network has at most <i>m/2 + 4η</i> stations. All but <i>12η</i> arrows lie in a core whose size is between <i>m/2 − 3η</i> and <i>m/2</i>. In particular, losing only a linear number of guarantees from the leading quadratic bound forces only a bounded number of exceptional stations and arrows. This is concentration of the original network; deleting the exceptions is not proved to preserve its score. See <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#quantitative-concentration-near-the-sparse-optimum">Theorem 60</a>.</p>
  <h2>Small cases, proof checks, and open questions</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Number of stations</th><th>Exact maximum <i>I</i></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1, 2, 3</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Every order from 12 onward</td><td><i>n(n − 3)</i></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p>The full joint tables through six stations come from exhaustive enumeration with an independent game checker. With arc count alone fixed, the exact maxima for zero through eleven arcs are 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, and 8, over every number of stations. The last budget before the uniform formulas is also exact: <strong><i>M<sub>E</sub>(23) = 83</i></strong>. A 12-station witness attains it; a degree reduction and three completed exhaustive checks prove that no network does better.</p>
  <p>For <strong>every <i>n ≥ 115</i> and every budget <i>m ≥ 24</i></strong>, the joint maximum is completely determined. Below <i>2n</i>, use the even or odd arc formula above and add isolated stations. From <i>2n</i> through <i>n(n − 3)</i>, every missing pair wins. The final dense staircase follows, and then the maximum is zero. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/proofs/#the-combined-joint-maximum">combined theorem</a> states every range precisely.</p>
  <figure><img src="/cop-prisoner/figures/joint-regimes.svg" alt="Three plots of the exact maximum at 1800 stations: quadratic sparse growth, linear decrease across the main interval, and the final dense decline to zero, with separately labeled axis scales" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The complete formula at 1800 stations. Separate scales make the sparse and dense endpoint regimes visible alongside the main interval.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>The remaining vertex bounds are <i>48 ≤ M<sub>V</sub>(9) ≤ 53</i>, <i>62 ≤ M<sub>V</sub>(10) ≤ 69</i>, and <i>84 ≤ M<sub>V</sub>(11) ≤ 87</i>. Other open questions include the remaining arc budgets from 12 through 22, the full joint maximum at moderate orders outside the proved intervals, and the structure of all maximizing graphs. A new ten-type construction can safely navigate in two independent directions. On simple four-regular quotient graphs of girth at least 133 and inverse-generator directed diameter D, eight recipient types have independently checked delivery within 32D + 64 moves. Types 2 and 7 remain open. This does not yet give universal logarithmic-time delivery at the optimal sparse budget; the detailed partial result and failed repairs are in the research archive.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/cop-prisoner/lean/">formal artifact</a> proves the actual game semantics, the connection from finite certificates to terminating strategies, structural lemmas, the complete 12-station sparse and 11-station dense examples, robust two-move envelopes including all six examples at orders 16 through 21, complementary-block two-move delivery, and preservation of protected routing under arbitrary changes to exterior arrows. It also verifies the staircase arithmetic from explicit counting hypotheses. The graph-to-counting argument and existence of the attaining blocks remain ordinary proofs. A checked component is not a formalization of every theorem.</p>
  <h2>Where the work comes from</h2>
  <p>The original problem and layered construction come from Alexandra Ignatova's 2023 research, mentored by Angel Raychev, and the retained February 2024 manuscript. This provenance is recorded in the <a href="https://uchi.bas.bg/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Knijka_reziumeta_2023.pdf">2023 student research abstracts, page 27</a>.</p>
  <p>Ignatova and Raychev had already established the upper bound <i>I(G) ≤ n(n − 3)</i> and pursued two-in/two-out constructions. The September 2026 work supplies the new attaining families, corrects the original layered counting argument, proves the needed balancing result, and develops the extremal bounds, new constructions, computational checks, and Lean formalization, with assistance from Astra 6 through the Codex harness. The <a href="/cop-prisoner/paper/">paper page</a> distinguishes these contributions and the remaining publication work.</p>
</Post>
